    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d157">

                <pb facs="0167" n="117" xml:id="pg117"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/1599</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Russia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Francis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vologda</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1918-04-10T22:00:00+02:31"><hi rendition="#italic">April 10, 1918,
                                10 p.m.</hi></date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received April 15, 5.35 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>87. For the Secretary:</p>
                <p>Robins<anchor n="1" xml:id="d157-f1"/> wires received following cable from
                        Thompson:<anchor n="2" xml:id="d157-f2"/> “Believe all relief you may ask
                    for will be furnished Russian Government.” Does this mean Red Cross supplies for
                    Russian people, between whom and government Thompson unable to distinguish, or
                    does it mean that our Government willing to aid Soviet government? If latter, I
                    respectfully, but positively, cannot endorse.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Francis</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d157-f1" n="1" xml:id="d157-f1-content">
                <p>Lieut. Col. Raymond Robins, then in charge of the American Red Cross Commission
                    to Russia.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d157-f2" n="2" xml:id="d157-f2-content">
                <p>Lieut. Col. William B. Thompson, in charge in 1917 of the American. Red; Cross
                    Commission to Russia.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
